#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180336} :activity/id #uuid "f75d5350-a041-5182-9b13-8e1a72d6079f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048639 :topic/id #uuid "b0e1b1ba-f451-5130-bf79-4959ebebab49" :topic/math-academy-id 3679} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180667 :tutorial/content "How can we subtract fractions, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{4} - \\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nSince both fractions have the same denominator ($4$), we can combine them into one fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{4} - \\frac{5}{4} &= \\frac{2 - 5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $2-5$ using our number line:\n\n![](images/c6/c63a3db44b79e751c7cf1a49dcebcbb1d00cea0427da4ef271d07646e4be2748.png)\n\nWe get $2-5=-3$, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{2 - 5}{4} = \\frac{-3}{4} =-\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator *first* and *then* subtract!" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0129df2-078e-5236-8584-b81858a690fe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3035 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4d9cc4ed-d42b-546d-ade4-504afd85f35a" :step/math-academy-id 16402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179559 :question/id #uuid "bcf37a88-0ac3-59ca-8d23-8075f3eab872" :question/math-academy-id "e-9399"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd087cbc-c76f-5900-a1ef-183c383e34ed" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050637}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180188}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting a Positive Fraction From a Fraction: Unlike Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "a1dacde5-4449-5189-978d-83b7ce04030f" :step/math-academy-id 35180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 175921